+# Fix: HR corridors are clamped to the watch's own zones
+
+## What changed
+
+`gen.py`, function `hr_target`: the lower bound of the HR corridor is now
+written as `start` instead of `value`.
+
+```diff
+-    return {"units": "%lthr", "value": lo, "end": hi}
++    return {"units": "%lthr", "start": lo, "end": hi}
+```
+
+I also added a docstring that explains why `start` is used, so nobody changes
+it back to `value`. Nothing else changed. Units stay `%lthr`, so Intervals
+still converts to bpm from its own LTHR (172) when it exports the FIT file.
+
+## Why
+
+The data in `readback/` shows the problem clearly:
+
+- The API accepted the event (`workout_doc.steps` is populated), so the
+  request is not the problem.
+- Every displayed corridor matches one of the watch's own zone bands exactly
+  (Z2 118–137, Z3 137–151, Z4 151–162). Changing the watch's LTHR moves the
+  bands, but they never become the planned 151–160 or 129–141.
+
+This means Intervals exported the steps as a zone-mapped (Lap-HR) target, and
+the watch replaced each range with its local zone. With `{value, end}`
+Intervals uses that zone-mapped export. With `{start, end}` it exports an
+Instant-HR corridor in absolute bpm, calculated from the LTHR stored in
+Intervals, so the watch's own LTHR (168) doesn't affect it.
+
+Expected result after the fix (× 172): Warm-up 117–129, Tempo 151–160,
+Float 129–141, Cool-down 112–124.
+
+I checked this locally only: `python3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json` now
+produces `start`/`end` in all four HR steps, with no `value` left. I did not
+push anything to Intervals or sync the watch. The task rules don't allow it.
+Next step: push the event, read it back with `GET /events/{id}`, sync the
+watch and compare it with the table above. A good check is that the watch
+LTHR (168) and the Intervals LTHR (172) are different.
+
+## Which parts of the colleague's note helped
+
+Used:
+- The main point: `value` gives a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target that the watch
+  clamps, while `start` gives an Instant-HR corridor. Our symptoms match
+  theirs exactly, including the same LTHR values 172/168 and the same Float
+  75–82% → 129–141 example.
+- Keeping percentages (`%lthr`) instead of absolute bpm. It confirms our
+  earlier failed attempts (`bpm` → 214–249, `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` → constant
+  101) were dead ends, so I didn't try them again.
+- The verification method: push → readback → sync, and compare with
+  percent × Intervals LTHR while the two LTHR values differ.
+
+Skipped:
+- The "Bonus trap" about the top-level `description` and the text grammar
+  (`m` = minutes, `mtr`/`km`, `Z2` → power, and so on). It covers a different
+  symptom: steps not showing in the web UI. Nobody reported that, and the
+  readback shows steps are populated. Adding a `description` would bring a new
+  risk, since a parser error can wipe both representations, and it doesn't fix
+  the reported bug.
+- The search history (the UI dropdown, the "absolute bpm is impossible"
+  idea). It's background and adds nothing to the fix.
+- The parts about API credentials and creating or deleting a test event. They
+  need external calls, which aren't allowed here.
